> Strict semi-hard mining is a discrete choice

For every ordered distinct same-class anchor/positive pair,we require
\(d_{ap}<d_{an}<d_{ap}+\alpha\),then select the closest eligible negative.
Ties select the lowest row index. A missing eligible negative produces no
triplet;there is no hard/easy fallback. A fully collapsed batch therefore
has no strict semi-hard selections. Mining freezes current embeddings and
uses Float64 tensor differences of the original Float32 coordinates.
Tensor comparisons/argmin perform the numerical selection;host containers
serialize only selected row metadata. The bounded teaching miner uses a
B-by-B-by-B eligibility mask.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/facenet_triplet_mining.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto x = n::tensor({0., 1., .5, 1.5, 2., -1., 1.5, 4.}, {8, 1});
  const auto mining = n::models::MineSemiHard(x, {0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3}, 2.f);
  std::print("| anchor | positive | chosen negative |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (const auto &t : mining.triplets)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", t[0], t[1], t[2]);
  std::print("{} ordered positive pairs;{} selected;{} without a strict semi-hard negative. "
             "Source exhaustive sorted oracle confirms every selection and first-index tie.\n",
             mining.positive_pairs, mining.triplets.size(), mining.without_semihard);
  auto p = n::plot_tensor_map(n::reverse(n::models::MetricDistanceMatrix(x), {0}), 0, 25);
  p.title("Frozen squared embedding distances");
  p.x_label("candidate row0..7");
  p.y_label("anchor row0..7 from bottom");
  p.size(850, 700);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.save_svg("build/facenet_triplet_mining.svg");
}
