\caption(Fixed fitting/validation triplet objectives and the varying mined-batch objective are plotted separately. Validation checkpoint selection precedes all test verification and retrieval scoring.)
\alttext(Fixed fitting/validation triplet objectives and the varying mined-batch objective are plotted separately. Validation checkpoint selection precedes all test verification and retrieval scoring.)

\image(figures/facenet_triplet_episodes.png)
\caption(First eight actual held-out3-way retrieval episodes:query then one support from each unseen class. The selected support has a green border when correct and red when wrong. Scores are negative squared128D embedding distances.)
\alttext(Actual held-out digits7–9 query/support retrieval episodes with correct and incorrect selected supports.)

The short run can reduce the fixed fitting objective much more than the
unseen-class objective. Its held-out accuracy and AUC describe this bounded
MNIST adaptation,not face recognition. An improvement over an untrained
baseline still leaves substantial error visible in the retrieval gallery.

> Conclusion

Tensor distances and strict mining preserve deterministic source selection rules without host numerical loops. Visible recomputation and Adam distinguish discrete selection from gradient flow. The full graph,independent derivatives,fixed probes and actual held-out images document both the mechanism and this small digit experiment’s generalization limits.

> References

- [Schroff,Kalenichenko and Philbin(2015),FaceNet:A Unified Embedding for Face Recognition and Clustering](https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.03832).
